// Practice quiz: Classes and objects
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/classes-and-objects/classes-and-objects

::o1 instance method::Class Rect has int fields w and h. Which method uses the w and h of the object it is called on? {
=int area() \{ return w * h; \}#Correct. An instance method reads the fields of the object it is called on.
~static int area() \{ return w * h; \}#Not quite. A static method belongs to the class, not to an object, so it cannot read instance fields directly.
~int area(int w, int h) \{ return 0; \}#Not quite. This takes its own parameters and returns 0; it never uses the fields.
~void area() \{ return w * h; \}#Not quite. A void method cannot return a value, so this does not compile.
}

::o1 field default::class Book \{ int pages; \} What is new Book().pages before anything assigns it? {
=0#Correct. Fields get a default value\: 0 for numbers, false for boolean and null for references.
~A compile error, because pages was never assigned#Not quite. That rule applies to local variables, not fields.
~null#Not quite. An int can never be null; null is the default only for reference fields.
~Whatever value was left in memory#Not quite. Java always gives fields a defined default value.
}

::o2 separate fields::class Dog \{ String name; \} Dog d1 \= new Dog(); Dog d2 \= new Dog(); d1.name \= "Rex"; What is d2.name? {
=null#Correct. d1 and d2 are two objects, each with its own name field; d2.name still has its default.
~"Rex"#Not quite. Only the object d1 refers to was changed.
~The empty String ""#Not quite. A String field starts as null, not as an empty String.
~A compile error#Not quite. Reading an unassigned field compiles; it holds its default value.
}

::o2 objects in a loop::A loop body runs three times, and each pass runs Dog d \= new Dog(); How many Dog objects are created? {
=3#Correct. new runs once per pass, so three separate objects are created.
~1, because there is only one variable d#Not quite. The variable is reused, but each new creates a fresh object.
~0, because d is never used#Not quite. Objects are created whether or not you use them afterwards.
~4#Not quite. The body runs three times, so new runs three times.
}

::o3 reference variable::In Car myCar \= new Car(); what is myCar? {
=A variable that holds a reference to a Car object#Correct. new Car() creates the object; myCar stores a reference to it.
~The class Car#Not quite. The class is the type written on the left, Car.
~The Car object itself#Not quite. The object lives on its own; the variable only refers to it.
~A call to the constructor#Not quite. new Car() is the constructor call; myCar is where its result is kept.
}

::o3 class role::Which statement about a class is true? {
=It describes the fields and methods that each of its objects will have#Correct. A class is the blueprint; objects are made from it with new while the program runs.
~A class can have only one object#Not quite. You can create as many objects of a class as you need.
~A class and its object are the same thing#Not quite. The class is the description; each object is a separate thing built from it.
~Assigning one variable to another copies the object#Not quite. Assignment copies the reference, so both variables refer to the same object.
}
